{"sentences": [{"sentence_id": "s1", "sentence": "Thirteen trials test CD19 CAR-T in myasthenia gravis (MG), with 11 currently running, one stopped before enrollment, and one with unknown status.", "cites": ["NCT06419166"]}, {"sentence_id": "s2", "sentence": "Among running trials, there is one Phase 2/3 trial (NCT06193889), one Phase 1/2 trial (NCT06359041), and nine Phase 1 or early Phase 1 trials.", "cites": ["NCT06419166"]}, {"sentence_id": "s3", "sentence": "Sponsors of active trials include Cabaletta Bio, Kyverna, BMS, Kite, TG Therapeutics, Zhejiang University, and several academic or hospital-based investigators.", "cites": ["NCT06419166"]}, {"sentence_id": "s4", "sentence": "One trial (NCT06419166) was withdrawn before enrolling any participants due to the study stopping early.", "cites": ["NCT06419166"]}, {"sentence_id": "s5", "sentence": "No CD19 CAR-T therapy has been approved for MG, and no trial terminations were due to safety concerns in this indication.", "cites": ["NCT06419166"]}, {"sentence_id": "s6", "sentence": "A 2026 case series in Cell Reports Medicine (PMID:42628528) reported three patients with refractory MG achieving sustained clinical remission for at least 19 months after anti-CD19 CAR T-cell therapy, allowing discontinuation of other immunotherapies.", "cites": ["PMID:42628528"]}, {"sentence_id": "s7", "sentence": "Recent reviews from 2026 (e.g., PMID:42936601, PMID:42824951) describe CD19 CAR-T as a promising strategy for deep and durable B-cell depletion in autoimmune neurologic diseases including MG.", "cites": ["PMID:42936601", "PMID:42824951"]}, {"sentence_id": "s8", "sentence": "The site's stop-risk model estimates a median 22.3% risk of termination across eight running CD19 CAR-T trials in MG, based on design and enrollment factors.", "cites": ["RISK:cd19-car-t:MG"]}, {"sentence_id": "s9", "sentence": "CD19 CAR-T is being explored alongside other advanced cellular and biologic therapies for MG, but early clinical data suggest potential for durable, treatment-free remission in refractory cases.", "cites": ["PMID:42628528"]}], "sources": [{"id": "NCT06419166", "text": "{"nct":"NCT06419166","title":"An Exploratory Clinical Study of GC012F Injection for Refractory gMG","state":"stopped","drug":"gc012f","start_year":2024,"end_year":2027,"phase":"EARLY_1","overall_status":"WITHDRAWN","why_stopped":"The study stopped early, before enrolling its first participant.","lead_sponsor":"Zhejiang University"}
{"row":{"id":"cd19-car-t","label":"CD19 CAR-T","gene":"CD19","blurb":"Engineered T cells that reset the B-cell compartment."},"trials_total":13,"counts_by_status":{"unknown":1,"running":11,"stopped":1},"counts_by_phase":{"Phase 1":11,"Phase 2":1,"Phase 3":1},"companies":{"Zhejiang University":2,"Kyverna":1,"BMS":1,"Cabaletta Bio":1,"Ting Chang, MD":1,"Xuanwu Hospital, Beijing":1,"TG Therapeutics":1,"Chongbo Zhao":1,"Changzhou No.2 People's Hospital":1,"Daishi Tian":1},"chain":{"family":"engineered T cells","drugs":["azercabtagene zapreleucel","caba-201","cc-97540","cd19 car-t cells","cd19-bcma targeted car-t dose 1","cd19/bcma-targeted car-t cells","gc012f","kite-363","kyv-101","universal allogeneic anti-cd19/bcma car t-cells","universal bcma car-t; universal cd19 car-t"],"approved":{},"related_targets":["cd19","cd20-car-t","bcma-car-t"]}}"}, {"id": "PMID:42628528", "text": "{"id":"PMID:42628528","title":"Long-term outcomes of anti-CD19 CAR T cell therapy in refractory myasthenia gravis: A case series.","year":"2026","journal":"Cell reports. Medicine","abstract":"Anti-CD19 CAR T cell therapy represents an emerging therapeutic approach for generalized, treatment-refractory myasthenia gravis (MG), a predominantly B-cell-mediated autoimmune disease for which durable treatment-free remission remains an unmet clinical goal. Despite recent advances, current targeted therapies generally require lifelong repeated administration and rarely induce durable treatment-free remission. We report on three patients with severe, treatment-resistant MG, including one patient with concomitant rheumatoid arthritis, treated with autologous, fully human anti-CD19 CAR T cells. All three patients achieve rapid, sustained clinical MG remission for at least 19 months, allowing discontinuation of MG-specific immunotherapies and substantial improvement in clinical and functional outcomes despite persistent detectable anti-AChR autoantibody titers. B cell depletion is profound, and treatment-related adverse events remain transient and manageable during long-term follow-up. These data support anti-CD19 CAR T cell therapy as a durable, effective intervention for refractory MG and warrant further evaluation in prospective controlled clinical trials."}
